 .\n\\]\nAny rectangular tile can contain **at most one** of the cells \\(\\{a_i\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA tile is a product of an interval of rows \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and an interval of columns \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nIf the tile contains \\(a_i\\) then \\(c_1\\le\\sigma(i)+1\\le c_2\\) and, because the tile must avoid the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), we must have \\(c_1>\\sigma(i)\\). Hence \\(c_1\\ge\\sigma(i)+1\\).  \nIf the same tile also contained \\(a_{i'}\\) for some \\(i'\\neq i\\), we would have simultaneously  \n\\(c_1\\ge\\sigma(i)+1\\) and \\(c_1\\ge\\sigma(i')+1\\); thus \\(c_1\\ge\\max\\{\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i')\\}+1\\).  \nConsequently the column \\(\\min\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\sigma(i')+1\\}\\) would be **strictly smaller** than \\(c_1\\) and could not belong to the tile. Hence the tile cannot contain both \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_{i'}\\). ∎\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); all other \\(n-1\\) rows satisfy \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\).  \nThus the set \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) distinct cells, each of which must be covered, and by Lemma 2.1 each tile can cover at most one of them.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the region \\(A\\).\n\nAn analogous argument applied to the cells immediately **above** each hole yields a symmetric lower bound for the region \\(B\\).\n\n##### Lemma 2.2  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)>1\\) define the cell  \n\\[\nb_j=(\\sigma^{-1}(j)-1,j)\\in B .\n\\]\nAny rectangular tile can contain at most one of the cells \\(\\{b_j\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The proof is identical to Lemma 2.1 after transposing the board (swap rows and columns). ∎\n\nExactly one column satisfies \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)=1\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns satisfy \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)>1\\).  \nHence at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover the region \\(B\\).\n\nCombining the two estimates we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles.}}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Upper‑triangular tiles.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k\\) has dimensions \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) and lies entirely in the set \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and their union equals the whole upper‑triangular region.\n\n*Lower‑triangular tiles.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_k\\) has dimensions \\((n-k+1)\\times (k-1)\\) and lies entirely in the set \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{S_k\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) is pairwise disjoint and together they cover the whole lower‑triangular region.\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other because every cell of an \\(R_k\\) satisfies \\(i\\le k<j\\) while every cell of an \\(S_k\\) satisfies \\(j\\le k-1<i\\).  \nConsequently the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_k\\}\\cup\\{S_k\\}\\) cover every cell of the board except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), which remain uncovered.  \nEach row and each column contains exactly one uncovered cell, namely the diagonal one.\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 3 achieves this number.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
