square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \n   - Define the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix \\(A=(a_{ij})\\) by  \n\n\\[\na_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0, & j=\\pi(i),\\\\[2pt]\n1, & j\\neq \\pi(i).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n   Thus \\(A=J-P\\), where \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound via matrix rank.**  \n   - A rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(R\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\) corresponds to the rank‑1 matrix \\(u_R v_C^{\\mathsf T}\\) (characteristic vectors of \\(R\\) and \\(C\\)).  \n   - Because tiles are disjoint, the matrix \\(A\\) is the sum of the rank‑1 matrices of all tiles. If \\(t\\) tiles are used, then  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(B_k)=t .\n\\]\n\n   - Prove \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).  \n     Suppose \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j\\,\\mathbf a_j=0\\), where \\(\\mathbf a_j\\) is column \\(j\\) of \\(A\\).  \n     For row \\(r\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j\\bigl(1-\\delta_{j,\\pi(r)}\\bigr)=0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{n}c_j\\Bigr)-c_{\\pi(r)}=0 .\n\\]\n\n     Hence \\(\\sum_{j}c_j=c_{\\pi(r)}\\) for every \\(r\\); because \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, all \\(c_j\\) are equal to a common value \\(S\\).  \n     Substituting back gives \\(nS-S=0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)S=0\\), so \\(S=0\\) and all \\(c_j=0\\).  \n     Therefore the columns of \\(A\\) are linearly independent and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).  \n   - Consequently any tiling must satisfy \\(t\\ge n\\). Thus at least \\(2025\\) tiles are necessary.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   - Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) place a single tile covering the whole row except the diagonal cell, i.e. the rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}\\bigr),\n\\]\n\n     which is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile.  \n   - The \\(n\