on attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) that describes the uncovered squares. For each row \\(i\\) place a single tile covering the whole row except the uncovered column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); formally the tile is  \n\n   \\[\n   R_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad C_i=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, cover every non‑hole cell, and each tile is a legitimate rectangle. Hence a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(t\\ge n\\) and the explicit construction with \\(t=n\\) together show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\). For the given board \\(n=2025\\), the answer is \\(\\boxed{2025}\\).\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles is required such that\n\n* each tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines,\n* no two tiles overlap (each unit square is covered by at most one tile),\n* in every row and in every column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nLet \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the bijection defined by  \n\\[\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\sigma(i)).\n\\]\nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \n\nAfter permuting the columns according to \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) we may assume that the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \nHence the set of squares that must be tiled is the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \na_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0,& i=j,\\\\[2pt]\n1,& i\\neq j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(A=J-I\\) where \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------