---------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rank lower bound for any tiling  \n\nSuppose the tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles with incidence matrices \\(B_1,\\dots ,B_t\\).  \nBecause the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the cells represented by \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t} B_k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor arbitrary matrices \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t}M_k\\Bigr)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(M_k).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to (4) and using \\(\\operatorname{rank}(B_k)\\le1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (multiplicity\u202f1) and \\(0\\) (multiplicity\u202f\\(n-1\\)).  \nConsequently the eigenvalues of \\(A=J-I\\) are  \n\n\\[\nn-1\\quad(\\text{multiplicity }1),\\qquad -1\\quad(\\text{multiplicity }n-1),\n\\]\n\nall non\u2011zero; therefore \\(A\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) and (6) we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction using exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nReturn to the original permutation \\(\\sigma\\) (before the column renaming).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad \nC_i=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe rectangle \\(R_i\\times C_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile that covers every cell of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nDifferent rows give disjoint tiles, and the union of all \\(n\\) tiles covers precisely the 1\u2011entries of matrix \\(A\\).  \nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (7) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles, while the construction (8) demonstrates that \\(n\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(n\\).\n\nFor the concrete board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
