> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(Q=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle (where \\(I,J\\) are non‑empty intervals of\n> rows and columns) that contains **no** hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n> Then \\(Q\\) contains **at most one** element of the set \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat three possibilities.\n\n1. **Two left cells.**  \n   Suppose \\(Q\\) contains \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\((i',\\sigma(i')-1)\\) with\n   \\(i\\neq i'\\).  Without loss assume \\(\\sigma(i)<\\sigma(i')\\).\n   Because both columns \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\) belong to the column\n   interval \\(J\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(i)\\le \\sigma(i')-1\\le \\max J .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) lies inside \\(Q\\), contradicting the\n   hypothesis.  Thus a rectangle cannot contain two left cells.\n\n2. **Two right cells.**  \n   This is symmetric: if \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) and \\((i',\\sigma(i')+1)\\) were both\n   in \\(Q\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i')\\), then the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) would\n   belong to \\(Q\\).  Hence at most one right cell can be inside a hole‑free\n   rectangle.\n\n3. **One left cell and one right cell.**  \n   Assume \\(Q\\) contains \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L\\) and \\((i',\\sigma(i')+1)\\in R\\).\n   The column interval \\(J\\) contains the two columns\n   \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')+1\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(i')+1\\) then \\(\\sigma(i)\\in J\\), so the hole\n   \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) lies in \\(Q\\).  \n   If \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i')+1\\) then \\(\\sigma(i')\\in J\\), so the hole\n   \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\) lies in \\(Q\\).  \n   In either case a hole is contained, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nThus a rectangle avoiding all holes can contain **no more than one** element of\n\\(L\\cup R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of rectangles\n\nEvery tile of the required tiling is a rectangle that contains no hole.\nBy the Lemma, each tile can cover at most one element of the set \\(L\\cup R\\).\nSince (1) shows that \\(|L\\cup R|=2n-2\\), any tiling must consist of at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).\nThe uncovered squares are precisely the main diagonal cells\n\\((i,i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nH_k=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(V_k\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-k)\\times1\\); it lies entirely to\n  the left of the hole \\((k,k)\\) and therefore contains no hole.\n* \\(H_k\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-k)\\); it lies entirely\n  above the hole \\((k,k)\\) and contains no hole.\n\nThe families \\(\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{H_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint, and together they cover every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\)\n(i.e., every cell that is not on the diagonal).  Hence we obtain a tiling with\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (2) and the explicit construction both give the same number\n\\(2n-2\\).  Therefore this number is the *minimum* possible.\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
